{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you register reusable components with "},{"type":"link","href":"./composable-tables","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"}]},{"type":"text","value":", those components read the current table, cell, or header from React context instead of from props. This guide explains how that context works, how to read it with full type-safety, and how to create an isolated context for advanced cases like nested tables."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-the-context-is-provided","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How The Context Is Provided"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The components returned by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppTable"},{"type":"text","value":" are what put values on context:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.AppTable>"},{"type":"text","value":" provides the current table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.AppCell cell={cell}>"},{"type":"text","value":" provides the current cell instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.AppHeader header={header}>"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.AppFooter header={footer}>"},{"type":"text","value":" provide the current header instance."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableComponents"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cellComponents"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headerComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" then read those instances with the matching hooks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext() // the table provided by <table.AppTable>\n  return <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n}\n\nfunction TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext<string>() // the cell provided by <table.AppCell>\n  return <span>{cell.getValue()}</span>\n}\n\nfunction SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext() // the header provided by <table.AppHeader>\n  return header.column.getIsSorted() ? '🔼' : null\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The instance you read back is the same one those wrappers render with, already extended with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"App*"},{"type":"text","value":" components and your registered components. For example, inside a table component "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.PaginationControls"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.AppCell"},{"type":"text","value":" are available, and inside a cell component "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.TextCell"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" are available."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Tip","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You often do not need table context to reach the table at all. Every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":" instance already carries a reference back to its table: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.table"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.table"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.table"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.table"},{"type":"text","value":". So inside a cell or header component you can read the table straight off the instance you already have (for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":") instead of calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTableContext()"},{"type":"text","value":". The instances are linked to each other too ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.row"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.column"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.column"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getAllCells()"},{"type":"text","value":"), so a single instance is usually enough to navigate to whatever you need. Reach for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTableContext()"},{"type":"text","value":" when a component has no instance to start from, such as a standalone toolbar or pagination control."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"import-the-hooks-from-your-createtablehook-result","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import The Hooks From Your createTableHook Result"}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the best type-safety, import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTableContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCellContext"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHeaderContext"},{"type":"text","value":" from the same module where you called "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":". Those hooks know your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"and"}]},{"type":"text","value":" your registered component maps, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.PaginationControls"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.TextCell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.SortIndicator"},{"type":"text","value":" are all typed."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// hooks/table.ts\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your tableComponents\n  useCellContext, //  <- typed with TFeatures + your cellComponents\n  useHeaderContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your headerComponents\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableComponents,\n  cellComponents,\n  headerComponents,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"passing-instances-through-your-own-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing Instances Through Your Own Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" is also useful on its own, separately from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":", for avoiding prop drilling. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":" instances are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stable references"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (the same stability TanStack Table relies on for React Compiler memoization). You can place any of them on a context and read it deep in a subtree, and because the reference does not change when table state changes, the context value stays the same and consumers will not re-render from state changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reach for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of hand-rolling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createContext"},{"type":"text","value":". It hands you a context plus a matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":"-typed hook, so you do not have to retype the value or write the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useContext"},{"type":"text","value":" guard yourself. (See "},{"type":"link","href":"#scoped-contexts-with-createtablehookcontexts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below for the other use of this util, passing the contexts into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":".)"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// cell-slot-context.ts\nexport const { cellContext, useCellContext } =\n  createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()\n\n// provide the (stable) cell once, near where you render it\nfunction CellSlot({ cell }: { cell: Cell<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  return (\n    <cellContext.Provider value={cell}>\n      <DeeplyNestedCellUI />\n    </cellContext.Provider>\n  )\n}\n\n// read it anywhere below, no prop drilling\nfunction DeeplyNestedCellUI() {\n  const cell = useCellContext() // typed with your TFeatures\n  return <span>{/* ... */}</span>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That stability is exactly why state-dependent method reads go stale. A component that reads something like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.column.getIsSorted()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.row.getIsSelected()"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.getValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" off a context-provided instance will not re-render when that state changes on its own. Wrap those method reads in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" render callback, or render directly from a value selected with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":", so the UI has an explicit reactive dependency. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./react-compiler#use-subscribe-in-nested-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use Subscribe in Nested Components"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" object is the exception. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a fresh React-facing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" reference whenever its selected state changes, which is intentional so that React Compiler invalidates JSX that depends on it. With the default selector that means every registered table-state change; a narrower selector only changes it for the selected updates. It is therefore "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a stable context value. Providing the table through your own context re-renders consumers whenever the providing component re-renders with a new wrapper. If you want a stable handle to pass down, provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" or a specific atom such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.rowSelection"},{"type":"text","value":" (these are stable) and read them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":", or keep the core table in a ref."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-default-shared-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Default Shared Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default you do not create or wire any context yourself. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" connects its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AppTable"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AppCell"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AppHeader"},{"type":"text","value":" providers to a shared, module-scoped context, and the hooks it returns read from that same context. Because the context lives at module scope, its identity is stable across hot module replacement during local development, so a component edit never leaves a provider and a consumer pointing at mismatched contexts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent tables stay isolated through normal provider scoping: each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.AppTable>"},{"type":"text","value":" provides its own table instance, and a consumer reads the nearest provider. Rendering two different tables side by side, or in different routes, works without any extra setup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scoped-contexts-with-createtablehookcontexts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The shared context only becomes a problem if you "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nest"}]},{"type":"text","value":" one table's provider inside another's and a consumer in that overlap reads the nearest (inner) provider instead of the one you intended. For that case, create an isolated context with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass it into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// scoped-table-context.ts (no component imports)\nimport { createTableHookContexts, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nexport const features = tableFeatures({/* ... */})\n\nexport const {\n  tableContext,\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"code","value":"// table.ts\nimport { createTableHook } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  cellContext,\n  features,\n  headerContext,\n  tableContext,\n} from './scoped-table-context'\n\nexport const { useAppTable } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableContext, // <- providers now use these scoped contexts\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  tableComponents: {/* ... */},\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each call to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a brand-new set of contexts, so two table setups that use their own scoped contexts can be nested without reading each other's values."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"which-hooks-to-use-with-scoped-contexts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which Hooks To Use With Scoped Contexts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" also returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTableContext"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCellContext"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHeaderContext"},{"type":"text","value":", but they are typed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" only. They do not know your registered component maps, because those are defined later in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":". So:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer the hooks returned from your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" call for the richest types (your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableComponents"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cellComponents"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headerComponents"},{"type":"text","value":" are typed)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the hooks from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" only when you need to read context from a module that cannot import the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" result."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This mirrors TanStack Form's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":", where the context hooks are intentionally loose and the strongly typed path lives on the form/field components."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-this","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When To Use This"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do nothing special for the common case. The default shared context handles single tables and multiple independent tables."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reach for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHookContexts"},{"type":"text","value":" only when you nest different table setups and need their contexts isolated, or when you want to read context from a component-free module."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-the-context-is-provided","text":"How The Context Is Provided","level":2},{"id":"import-the-hooks-from-your-createtablehook-result","text":"Import The Hooks From Your createTableHook Result","level":2},{"id":"passing-instances-through-your-own-context","text":"Passing Instances Through Your Own Context","level":2},{"id":"the-default-shared-context","text":"The Default Shared Context","level":2},{"id":"scoped-contexts-with-createtablehookcontexts","text":"Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts","level":2},{"id":"which-hooks-to-use-with-scoped-contexts","text":"Which Hooks To Use With Scoped Contexts","level":3},{"id":"when-to-use-this","text":"When To Use This","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nWhen you register reusable components with [`createTableHook`](./composable-tables), those components read the current table, cell, or header from React context instead of from props. This guide explains how that context works, how to read it with full type-safety, and how to create an isolated context for advanced cases like nested tables.\n\n## How The Context Is Provided\n\nThe components returned by `useAppTable` are what put values on context:\n\n- `<table.AppTable>` provides the current table instance.\n- `<table.AppCell cell={cell}>` provides the current cell instance.\n- `<table.AppHeader header={header}>` and `<table.AppFooter header={footer}>` provide the current header instance.\n\nYour registered `tableComponents`, `cellComponents`, and `headerComponents` then read those instances with the matching hooks:\n\n```tsx\nfunction PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext() // the table provided by <table.AppTable>\n  return <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n}\n\nfunction TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext<string>() // the cell provided by <table.AppCell>\n  return <span>{cell.getValue()}</span>\n}\n\nfunction SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext() // the header provided by <table.AppHeader>\n  return header.column.getIsSorted() ? '🔼' : null\n}\n```\n\nThe instance you read back is the same one those wrappers render with, already extended with the `App*` components and your registered components. For example, inside a table component `table.PaginationControls` and `table.AppCell` are available, and inside a cell component `cell.TextCell` and `cell.FlexRender` are available.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> You often do not need table context to reach the table at all. Every `cell`, `header`, `row`, and `column` instance already carries a reference back to its table: `cell.table`, `header.table`, `row.table`, and `column.table`. So inside a cell or header component you can read the table straight off the instance you already have (for example `cell.table.nextPage()`) instead of calling `useTableContext()`. The instances are linked to each other too (`cell.row`, `cell.column`, `header.column`, `row.getAllCells()`), so a single instance is usually enough to navigate to whatever you need. Reach for `useTableContext()` when a component has no instance to start from, such as a standalone toolbar or pagination control.\n\n## Import The Hooks From Your createTableHook Result\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For the best type-safety, import `useTableContext`, `useCellContext`, and `useHeaderContext` from the same module where you called `createTableHook`. Those hooks know your `TFeatures` **and** your registered component maps, so `table.PaginationControls`, `cell.TextCell`, and `header.SortIndicator` are all typed.\n\n```tsx\n// hooks/table.ts\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your tableComponents\n  useCellContext, //  <- typed with TFeatures + your cellComponents\n  useHeaderContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your headerComponents\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableComponents,\n  cellComponents,\n  headerComponents,\n})\n```\n\n## Passing Instances Through Your Own Context\n\n`createTableHookContexts` is also useful on its own, separately from `createTableHook`, for avoiding prop drilling. The `column`, `row`, `cell`, and `header` instances are **stable references** (the same stability TanStack Table relies on for React Compiler memoization). You can place any of them on a context and read it deep in a subtree, and because the reference does not change when table state changes, the context value stays the same and consumers will not re-render from state changes.\n\nReach for `createTableHookContexts` instead of hand-rolling `createContext`. It hands you a context plus a matching `TFeatures`-typed hook, so you do not have to retype the value or write the `useContext` guard yourself. (See [Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts](#scoped-contexts-with-createtablehookcontexts) below for the other use of this util, passing the contexts into `createTableHook`.)\n\n```tsx\n// cell-slot-context.ts\nexport const { cellContext, useCellContext } =\n  createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()\n\n// provide the (stable) cell once, near where you render it\nfunction CellSlot({ cell }: { cell: Cell<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  return (\n    <cellContext.Provider value={cell}>\n      <DeeplyNestedCellUI />\n    </cellContext.Provider>\n  )\n}\n\n// read it anywhere below, no prop drilling\nfunction DeeplyNestedCellUI() {\n  const cell = useCellContext() // typed with your TFeatures\n  return <span>{/* ... */}</span>\n}\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> That stability is exactly why state-dependent method reads go stale. A component that reads something like `header.column.getIsSorted()`, `cell.row.getIsSelected()`, or `cell.getValue()` off a context-provided instance will not re-render when that state changes on its own. Wrap those method reads in a `Subscribe` render callback, or render directly from a value selected with `useSelector`, so the UI has an explicit reactive dependency. See [Use Subscribe in Nested Components](./react-compiler#use-subscribe-in-nested-components).\n\nThe `table` object is the exception. `useTable` returns a fresh React-facing `table` reference whenever its selected state changes, which is intentional so that React Compiler invalidates JSX that depends on it. With the default selector that means every registered table-state change; a narrower selector only changes it for the selected updates. It is therefore **not** a stable context value. Providing the table through your own context re-renders consumers whenever the providing component re-renders with a new wrapper. If you want a stable handle to pass down, provide `table.store` or a specific atom such as `table.atoms.rowSelection` (these are stable) and read them with `Subscribe` / `useSelector`, or keep the core table in a ref.\n\n## The Default Shared Context\n\nBy default you do not create or wire any context yourself. `createTableHook` connects its `AppTable`/`AppCell`/`AppHeader` providers to a shared, module-scoped context, and the hooks it returns read from that same context. Because the context lives at module scope, its identity is stable across hot module replacement during local development, so a component edit never leaves a provider and a consumer pointing at mismatched contexts.\n\nIndependent tables stay isolated through normal provider scoping: each `<table.AppTable>` provides its own table instance, and a consumer reads the nearest provider. Rendering two different tables side by side, or in different routes, works without any extra setup.\n\n## Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts\n\nThe shared context only becomes a problem if you **nest** one table's provider inside another's and a consumer in that overlap reads the nearest (inner) provider instead of the one you intended. For that case, create an isolated context with `createTableHookContexts` and pass it into `createTableHook`:\n\n```tsx\n// scoped-table-context.ts (no component imports)\nimport { createTableHookContexts, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nexport const features = tableFeatures({/* ... */})\n\nexport const {\n  tableContext,\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()\n```\n\n```tsx\n// table.ts\nimport { createTableHook } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  cellContext,\n  features,\n  headerContext,\n  tableContext,\n} from './scoped-table-context'\n\nexport const { useAppTable } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableContext, // <- providers now use these scoped contexts\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  tableComponents: {/* ... */},\n})\n```\n\nEach call to `createTableHookContexts` returns a brand-new set of contexts, so two table setups that use their own scoped contexts can be nested without reading each other's values.\n\n### Which Hooks To Use With Scoped Contexts\n\n`createTableHookContexts` also returns `useTableContext`/`useCellContext`/`useHeaderContext`, but they are typed with `TFeatures` only. They do not know your registered component maps, because those are defined later in `createTableHook`. So:\n\n- Prefer the hooks returned from your `createTableHook` call for the richest types (your `tableComponents`/`cellComponents`/`headerComponents` are typed).\n- Use the hooks from `createTableHookContexts` only when you need to read context from a module that cannot import the `createTableHook` result.\n\nThis mirrors TanStack Form's `createFormHookContexts`, where the context hooks are intentionally loose and the strongly typed path lives on the form/field components.\n\n## When To Use This\n\n- Do nothing special for the common case. The default shared context handles single tables and multiple independent tables.\n- Reach for `createTableHookContexts` only when you nest different table setups and need their contexts isolated, or when you want to read context from a component-free module.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Table Context Guide\n---\n\nWhen you register reusable components with [`createTableHook`](./composable-tables), those components read the current table, cell, or header from React context instead of from props. This guide explains how that context works, how to read it with full type-safety, and how to create an isolated context for advanced cases like nested tables.\n\n## How The Context Is Provided\n\nThe components returned by `useAppTable` are what put values on context:\n\n- `<table.AppTable>` provides the current table instance.\n- `<table.AppCell cell={cell}>` provides the current cell instance.\n- `<table.AppHeader header={header}>` and `<table.AppFooter header={footer}>` provide the current header instance.\n\nYour registered `tableComponents`, `cellComponents`, and `headerComponents` then read those instances with the matching hooks:\n\n```tsx\nfunction PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext() // the table provided by <table.AppTable>\n  return <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n}\n\nfunction TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext<string>() // the cell provided by <table.AppCell>\n  return <span>{cell.getValue()}</span>\n}\n\nfunction SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext() // the header provided by <table.AppHeader>\n  return header.column.getIsSorted() ? '🔼' : null\n}\n```\n\nThe instance you read back is the same one those wrappers render with, already extended with the `App*` components and your registered components. For example, inside a table component `table.PaginationControls` and `table.AppCell` are available, and inside a cell component `cell.TextCell` and `cell.FlexRender` are available.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> You often do not need table context to reach the table at all. Every `cell`, `header`, `row`, and `column` instance already carries a reference back to its table: `cell.table`, `header.table`, `row.table`, and `column.table`. So inside a cell or header component you can read the table straight off the instance you already have (for example `cell.table.nextPage()`) instead of calling `useTableContext()`. The instances are linked to each other too (`cell.row`, `cell.column`, `header.column`, `row.getAllCells()`), so a single instance is usually enough to navigate to whatever you need. Reach for `useTableContext()` when a component has no instance to start from, such as a standalone toolbar or pagination control.\n\n## Import The Hooks From Your createTableHook Result\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For the best type-safety, import `useTableContext`, `useCellContext`, and `useHeaderContext` from the same module where you called `createTableHook`. Those hooks know your `TFeatures` **and** your registered component maps, so `table.PaginationControls`, `cell.TextCell`, and `header.SortIndicator` are all typed.\n\n```tsx\n// hooks/table.ts\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your tableComponents\n  useCellContext, //  <- typed with TFeatures + your cellComponents\n  useHeaderContext, // <- typed with TFeatures + your headerComponents\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableComponents,\n  cellComponents,\n  headerComponents,\n})\n```\n\n## Passing Instances Through Your Own Context\n\n`createTableHookContexts` is also useful on its own, separately from `createTableHook`, for avoiding prop drilling. The `column`, `row`, `cell`, and `header` instances are **stable references** (the same stability TanStack Table relies on for React Compiler memoization). You can place any of them on a context and read it deep in a subtree, and because the reference does not change when table state changes, the context value stays the same and consumers will not re-render from state changes.\n\nReach for `createTableHookContexts` instead of hand-rolling `createContext`. It hands you a context plus a matching `TFeatures`-typed hook, so you do not have to retype the value or write the `useContext` guard yourself. (See [Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts](#scoped-contexts-with-createtablehookcontexts) below for the other use of this util, passing the contexts into `createTableHook`.)\n\n```tsx\n// cell-slot-context.ts\nexport const { cellContext, useCellContext } =\n  createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()\n\n// provide the (stable) cell once, near where you render it\nfunction CellSlot({ cell }: { cell: Cell<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  return (\n    <cellContext.Provider value={cell}>\n      <DeeplyNestedCellUI />\n    </cellContext.Provider>\n  )\n}\n\n// read it anywhere below, no prop drilling\nfunction DeeplyNestedCellUI() {\n  const cell = useCellContext() // typed with your TFeatures\n  return <span>{/* ... */}</span>\n}\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> That stability is exactly why state-dependent method reads go stale. A component that reads something like `header.column.getIsSorted()`, `cell.row.getIsSelected()`, or `cell.getValue()` off a context-provided instance will not re-render when that state changes on its own. Wrap those method reads in a `Subscribe` render callback, or render directly from a value selected with `useSelector`, so the UI has an explicit reactive dependency. See [Use Subscribe in Nested Components](./react-compiler#use-subscribe-in-nested-components).\n\nThe `table` object is the exception. `useTable` returns a fresh React-facing `table` reference whenever its selected state changes, which is intentional so that React Compiler invalidates JSX that depends on it. With the default selector that means every registered table-state change; a narrower selector only changes it for the selected updates. It is therefore **not** a stable context value. Providing the table through your own context re-renders consumers whenever the providing component re-renders with a new wrapper. If you want a stable handle to pass down, provide `table.store` or a specific atom such as `table.atoms.rowSelection` (these are stable) and read them with `Subscribe` / `useSelector`, or keep the core table in a ref.\n\n## The Default Shared Context\n\nBy default you do not create or wire any context yourself. `createTableHook` connects its `AppTable`/`AppCell`/`AppHeader` providers to a shared, module-scoped context, and the hooks it returns read from that same context. Because the context lives at module scope, its identity is stable across hot module replacement during local development, so a component edit never leaves a provider and a consumer pointing at mismatched contexts.\n\nIndependent tables stay isolated through normal provider scoping: each `<table.AppTable>` provides its own table instance, and a consumer reads the nearest provider. Rendering two different tables side by side, or in different routes, works without any extra setup.\n\n## Scoped Contexts With createTableHookContexts\n\nThe shared context only becomes a problem if you **nest** one table's provider inside another's and a consumer in that overlap reads the nearest (inner) provider instead of the one you intended. For that case, create an isolated context with `createTableHookContexts` and pass it into `createTableHook`:\n\n```tsx\n// scoped-table-context.ts (no component imports)\nimport { createTableHookContexts, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nexport const features = tableFeatures({/* ... */})\n\nexport const {\n  tableContext,\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHookContexts<typeof features>()\n```\n\n```tsx\n// table.ts\nimport { createTableHook } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  cellContext,\n  features,\n  headerContext,\n  tableContext,\n} from './scoped-table-context'\n\nexport const { useAppTable } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n  tableContext, // <- providers now use these scoped contexts\n  cellContext,\n  headerContext,\n  tableComponents: {/* ... */},\n})\n```\n\nEach call to `createTableHookContexts` returns a brand-new set of contexts, so two table setups that use their own scoped contexts can be nested without reading each other's values.\n\n### Which Hooks To Use With Scoped Contexts\n\n`createTableHookContexts` also returns `useTableContext`/`useCellContext`/`useHeaderContext`, but they are typed with `TFeatures` only. They do not know your registered component maps, because those are defined later in `createTableHook`. So:\n\n- Prefer the hooks returned from your `createTableHook` call for the richest types (your `tableComponents`/`cellComponents`/`headerComponents` are typed).\n- Use the hooks from `createTableHookContexts` only when you need to read context from a module that cannot import the `createTableHook` result.\n\nThis mirrors TanStack Form's `createFormHookContexts`, where the context hooks are intentionally loose and the strongly typed path lives on the form/field components.\n\n## When To Use This\n\n- Do nothing special for the common case. The default shared context handles single tables and multiple independent tables.\n- Reach for `createTableHookContexts` only when you nest different table setups and need their contexts isolated, or when you want to read context from a component-free module.\n"}
